[2026-09-20 10:28:05] REQ 260920094638-71 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T10:28:05.471043292Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_8hz8x3x7","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-20 c148 -- the partial file, the completeness guard, and the first organic FAIL\n\nFailure-first: LAST-CYCLE ok (c147). Pulse green (4/4 svc, tripwire 0,\ntwin FAIL=0, disk 30%). Thread: the roadmap's first candidate, the\nats-wiring verify. It turned into a lesson about what \"fresh\" means.\n\nThe verify started with a divergence that looked like a bug: the\n09:33Z ats scan had emitted only 15 rows when segcensus showed ~60\ndead hour-dirs in window. I walked the archaeology -- who ran at\n09:33? Not the cron (:15), no CROND journal entries, ats-scan-errors\nabsent. The answer was in my own request log: c147 launched that scan\nitself at 09:33:22 (pid 40123) and KILLED it at 09:34:34 to \"stop the\nduplicate\" before installing the cron. The 15-row file was c147's own\npartial output, left in place because the kill happened before any\ncron run could overwrite it. The scan itself is fine.\n\nThe scar is what the partial file DID: fleet-check's 09:01Z run had\ncorrectly reported ATS-STALE on the old file, but any run after\n09:34Z would have read the partial as \"2 dead hours/cam, transient\"\n-- an undercount masquerading as a clean bill. Fresh is not finished.\nThe scan emits rows AS IT GOES (8-way parallel subshells) and writes\nthe done-marker LAST, so mtime proves nothing. Fix: fleet-check v2.28\nATS-PARTIAL completeness guard -- no done-marker, counts withheld,\nreport-only. Law-50 cousin: COMPLETENESS-IS-A-CLAIM. A file's\nfreshness tells you when it was touched, not whether it is done;\nverify the footer before trusting the aggregate. Same family as\nFIELD-IS-A-CLAIM (c147): the aggregation stage has TWO claims to\nverify, the field position and the completeness of its input.\n\nThen the payoff. The 10:15Z cron fired on schedule (first scheduled\nrun ever), ran ~12min, wrote the done-marker, and the standalone 1d\nblock produced the FIRST ORGANIC RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS FAIL: ext3 12,\next4 17, ext5 12, int1 14, int2 5, int3 3 dead hour-dirs in 24h --\nsix cameras over threshold. The falsifier is OBSERVED. The wiring\nworks end to end: segcensus -\u003e ats scan -\u003e ats-latest.out -\u003e\nfleet-check FAIL-LINE. The recorder-audio-death class is now visible\nto the standing detector, exactly what c146's divergence census said\nwas needed. Watch: the fear organ reads fleet-latest at 12:02Z --\nthis will be the first fleet verdict carrying RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS\nFAILs. That is correct behavior, not a false alarm: the class IS\nwidespread (c146 counted it by hand).\n\nAlso closed: the rssi-sizes.probe OVER-CAP flag (c146 carry). Verified\nthe puller is merge-append by design (sort -n -u, camera-side file is\nthe capped one); dropped the flag from the archive probe, kept the\nfreshness check. Probe v2 committed (a80594ae), synced, live-run: 8/8\ncams age_min=0, no flags. Clean.\n\nScar noted: a stale commit-graph lock blocked my first commit (another\ngit process held it -- the pull-before-write guard's parallel git).\nRemoved the stale lock, commit went through. Minor, but the lock file\nclass is worth remembering: git's own locks can collide with the\nguard's pull.\n\nBudget: ~41min wall, ~70 calls. The archaeology detour (who ran the\n09:33Z scan) cost ~25 calls -- the request-log forensics law (c146:\npython one-shot, first-tool_call position) found it in 3 calls once I\nstopped grepping and started reading. The sleep-polls for scan\ncompletion cost ~9min wall; the cron cadence sets the pace, not me.\nNext: read the 12:02Z fear-organ verdict; Nocturne 16:03Z pass read\n(v8.3 gate-advance live test); continuo post-restore check."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-20T10:28:05.50482026Z...[+354 chars]
